      "behaviour": "answer",
      "question": "How should I punctuate a parenthetic expression in the middle of a sentence?",
      "answer": "Enclose a parenthetic expression between commas (or, at the end of a sentence, between the comma and the period). If the parenthetic expression is preceded by a conjunction, place the first comma before the conjunction, not after it. If the interruption is very slight you may omit the commas, but never insert one comma and omit the other. Certain items\u2014years in dates, the day of the month after a weekday, the abbreviations etc. and jr., and non\u2011restrictive relative clauses\u2014are always set off by commas. (Rule 3)",
      "sources": [
