      "id": "u-comma-splice",
      "category": "usage",
      "behaviour": "answer",
      "question": "Can I join two independent clauses with just a comma?",
      "answer": "No. Do not join independent clauses with just a comma. Use a semicolon (or make two sentences), or use a comma only when a coordinating conjunction is also present. If the second clause begins with an adverb such as accordingly, then, therefore, or thus and no conjunction is used, a semicolon is required. Exceptions: very short, balanced clauses (Man proposes, God disposes) and certain colloquial dialogue (I hardly knew him, he was so changed), though the latter is inappropriate in formal writing. (Rule 5)",
      "sources": [
